    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-f/q5",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-f/q5-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The battery lifetime of a certain make of laptop is claimed to be Normally distributed 2 with a mean of 6.6 hours and variance of  3.9 hours . The battery lifetimes of forty such laptops were measured and the mean was 6.1 hours. Determine, at the 10% level of significance, whether the claim on the battery lifetime of the laptop is justified. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-f/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let µ be the population mean lifetime in hours: Under the stated normal model with variance 3.9, test H0 : µ = 6.6 [B1] against H1 : µ̸ = 6.6. [A1] The standard error of the sample mean is p 3.9/40, so z = 6.1 −6.6 3.9/40 ≈−1.6013. [A1] p\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-f/q5-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The lower critical value is Φ−1(0.05) = −1.64485 . . . and the upper is its positive counterpart, leaving 0.05 in each tail: The two-tailed 10% region is |z| &gt; 1.6449. [A1] The statistic is outside that region, and the two-tailed p-value is about 0.1093. Fail to reject H0. [A1] There is insufficient evidence at the 10% level that the population mean differs from 6.6 hours: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 5 is 6 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The battery lifetime of a certain make of laptop is claimed to be Normally distributed 2 with a mean of 6.6 hours and variance of  3.9 hours . The battery lifetimes of forty such laptops were measured and the mean was 6.1 hours. Determine, at the 10% level of significance, whether the claim on the battery lifetime of the laptop is justified. ( ) 6"
  },
